  "title": "Access List Items",
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "Course: Python Numpy for Data Science"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 1,
      "content": "Access List Items"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Lists are ordered. It means that the items of a list are always in the specified order unless we change it."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Suppose we have the following list:"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "python",
      "content": "number = [10, 12, 15]"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Here,"
    },
    {
      "type": "bullet_list",
      "items": [
        "The first item of this list is 10",
        "The second item is 12",
        "The third item is 15"
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "To access individual items from the list, we use their index. Python indexing starts at 0. So, in the above list:"
    },
    {
      "type": "bullet_list",
      "items": [
        "The index of the first item is 0",
        "The index of the second item is 1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "The index of the third item is 2"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "And, we can access individual items of the list by placing their respective index inside square brackets."
    },
    {
      "type": "image",
      "alt": "How the indexing of the list works.",
      "src": "data:image/png;base64,"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Source Code"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "python",
      "content": "1numbers = [10, 12, 15]\n2\n3# access first item\n4first_number = numbers[0]\n5print(first_number)   # 10\n6\n7# access second item\n8print(numbers[1])   # 12\n9\n10# access third item\n11print(numbers[2])   # 15"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Now, let's see what will happen if we try to access the item at index 3."
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "python",
      "content": "1numbers = [10, 12, 15]\n2\n3# trying to access the fourth item using index 3\n4print(numbers[3])"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Output"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "text",
      "content": "IndexError: list index out of range"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Our numbers list has only 3 elements. If we try to access the fourth element using index 3, we will get an error."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "content": "How does it work?"
    },
